0
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:07,040
Let's consider a one dimensional motion that

1
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,910
has a non-uniform acceleration.

2
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:11,530
What we'd like to do is explore how

3
00:00:11,530 --> 00:00:13,800
do you differentiate position functions,

4
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:17,270
to get velocity functions, to get acceleration functions.

5
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:19,230
So what we're going to consider is a rocket.

6
00:00:19,230 --> 00:00:21,970
So I'm going to choose a coordinate system y.

7
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:23,350
And here's my rocket.

8
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:26,380
And I have a function y of t.

9
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:29,150
And I'll have a j-hat direction, but this will

10
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:32,000
be a one dimensional motion.

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,280
Now I want to express while the rocket is thrusting upwards

12
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:41,100
and the engine is burning, we can describe a function y of t

13
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:45,626
to be equal to 1/2 a constant a naught

14
00:00:45,626 --> 00:00:49,065
minus the gravitational acceleration, times t squared.

15
00:00:49,065 --> 00:00:51,190
And we're going to have a separate term here, which

16
00:00:51,190 --> 00:00:52,950
is minus 1 over 30.

17
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:54,540
And you'll see where this 30 comes in

18
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:56,210
as we start to differentiate.

19
00:00:56,210 --> 00:01:01,360
The same constant a naught, t to the 1/6 over t naught

20
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:02,570
to the 1/4.

21
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:06,960
Now in this expression, a naught is bigger than g.

22
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:09,580
And also, this is only true, this

23
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:12,870
holds for the time interval 0 less than

24
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:16,190
or equal to t, less than t naught.

25
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:25,680
And at time t equals to t naught, the engine shuts off.

26
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,610
And at that moment, our expectation

27
00:01:27,610 --> 00:01:30,800
will be that the y component of the acceleration

28
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:37,760
should just be minus g, for t greater than t naught.

29
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:41,910
So now let's calculate the acceleration

30
00:01:41,910 --> 00:01:46,030
as the velocity and the position as functions of time.

31
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:48,020
So the velocity-- in each case, we're

32
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:49,810
going to use the polynomial rule.

33
00:01:49,810 --> 00:01:52,180
So the y component of the velocity

34
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:56,420
is just the derivative of t squared, which is just 2t.

35
00:01:56,420 --> 00:02:00,990
And so we get a naught minus g times t.

36
00:02:00,990 --> 00:02:05,340
And when we differentiate t at the 1/6, the 6 over 30

37
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:07,090
gives us factor 1 over 5.

38
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:13,620
So we have minus 1 over 5 times a naught, t to the 1/5

39
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:15,960
over t naught to the 1/4.

40
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:19,910
And this is a combination of a linear term and a term

41
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:23,260
that is decreasing by this t to the 1/5 factor.

42
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:27,080
And finally, we now take the next derivative, ay of t,

43
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:31,100
which is d dy dt.

44
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:34,160
I'll just keep functions of t, but we don't really need that.

45
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:38,350
And when we differentiate here, we get a naught minus g.

46
00:02:38,350 --> 00:02:40,750
Now you see the 5s are canceling,

47
00:02:40,750 --> 00:02:45,350
and we have minus a naught t to the 1/4 over t naught

48
00:02:45,350 --> 00:02:46,840
to the 1/4.

49
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:52,050
Now at time t equals t naught, what do we have?

50
00:02:52,050 --> 00:02:56,660
Well, ay at t equals t naught.

51
00:02:56,660 --> 00:02:58,460
This is just a factor minus a naught.

52
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:03,730
Those cancel, and we get minus g, which is what we expected.

